{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KGZADFRopWU63pFvhQGRiD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n2\t# run.sh — the run registry: the run-status record, registry, control, worker\n3\t# health, and the hub's read views, owned in one module (per CONTEXT.md's module\n4\t# map). loop-core.sh (the old god-module) is deleted; this is where its registry/\n5\t# control/worker-health/hub-view concerns now live (the worker orchestration /\n6\t# background fan-out lives in lib/worker.sh).\n7\t#\n8\t# A run-status *record* is a per-run status.tsv — one `key<TAB>value` line per\n9\t# field. This module owns the CANONICAL FIELD LIST and the only set/get-by-name\n10\t# accessors for it, so the run-status contract — every run (ralph or harden)\n11\t# carries exactly this key set, in this order — is enforced *by construction*:\n12\t# a caller names only the fields it has values for; the record fills in the rest,\n13\t# and no writer ever enumerates the schema by hand.\n14\t#\n15\t# No lib/core.sh dependency (uses only printf/read/awk/etc), so the record +\n16\t# registry are their own test surface — tests/test-run.sh sources this file\n17\t# directly. The hub read-views compose the gum-or-plain UI seam (lib/ui.sh) and\n18\t# the per-run control dispatches each loop's signal files through its adapter\n19\t# (lib/loops.sh), so source those two idempotently — they are self-contained\n20\t# (no core.sh) too. pwd -P resolves the install symlink so the siblings are found\n21\t# from either path.\n22\tif ! declare -F almanac_loop_ui_render >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n23\t  __run_dir=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n24\t  # shellcheck source=lib/ui.sh\n25\t  source \"$__run_dir/ui.sh\"\n26\t  unset __run_dir\n27\tfi\n28\t\n29\tif ! declare -F almanac_loop_adapter_call >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n30\t  __run_dir=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n31\t  # shellcheck source=lib/loops.sh\n32\t  source \"$__run_dir/loops.sh\"\n33\t  unset __run_dir\n34\tfi\n35\t\n36\t# THE single source of truth for the run-status schema: the canonical field list\n37\t# in write order. record_set iterates exactly this list, so every record carries\n38\t# the same keys regardless of which subset a caller supplies. The first seven are\n39\t# also the registry's index.tsv columns (the lightweight list pointer); the last\n40\t# three (finished_at/round/summary) are the per-run detail the index omits.\n41\talmanac_loop_record_fields() {\n42\t  printf '%s\\n' \\\n43\t    id type target pid status_file started_at status finished_at round summary failure_reason \\\n44\t    provider model effort iterations oversee lenses rounds queue_progress\n45\t}\n46\t\n47\t# Get one field from a record FILE by name. Prints the value (which may itself\n48\t# contain tabs — reassembled from the trailing columns) and returns 0; returns 1\n49\t# when the field is absent. This is the generic tab-field reader for the run\n50\t# registry; almanac_loop_status_field (below) is a thin alias over it for the\n51\t# worker-status and hub read paths.\n52\talmanac_loop_record_get() {\n53\t  local file=\"$1\" wanted=\"$2\" key value rest\n54\t\n55\t  while IFS=$'\\t' read -r key value rest; do\n56\t    if [ \"$key\" = \"$wanted\" ]; then\n57\t      if [ -n \"${rest:-}\" ]; then\n58\t        value=\"${value}\"$'\\t'\"${rest}\"\n59\t      fi\n60\t      printf '%s\\n' \"$value\"\n61\t      return 0\n62\t    fi\n63\t  done < \"$file\"\n64\t\n65\t  return 1\n66\t}\n67\t\n68\t# Generic TSV record engine: rewrite FILE with the schema in FIELDS_TEXT, in\n69\t# canonical order, with each (existing) field preserved unless overridden by a\n70\t# `field=value` PAIR. This is the deep module that owns the round-trip algorithm\n71\t# — schema-specific wrappers (record_set for run-status, worker_record_set for\n72\t# worker-status) just supply their own field list. Returns 2 on a missing file\n73\t# argument or a pair whose field isn't in FIELDS_TEXT.\n74\talmanac_loop_tsv_record_set() {\n75\t  local fields_text=\"$1\" file=\"$2\"\n76\t  shift 2 || return 2\n77\t  [ -n \"$file\" ] || return 2\n78\t\n79\t  local pair pfield field cur val out=\"\"\n80\t  local have_file=0\n81\t  if [ -f \"$file\" ]; then\n82\t    have_file=1\n83\t  fi\n84\t\n85\t  for pair in \"$@\"; do\n86\t    pfield=\"${pair%%=*}\"\n87\t    almanac_loop_tsv_has_field \"$fields_text\" \"$pfield\" || return 2\n88\t  done\n89\t\n90\t  while IFS= read -r field; do\n91\t    [ -n \"$field\" ] || continue\n92\t    cur=\"\"\n93\t    if [ \"$have_file\" = 1 ]; then\n94\t      cur=\"$(almanac_loop_record_get \"$file\" \"$field\" || true)\"\n95\t    fi\n96\t    val=\"$cur\"\n97\t    for pair in \"$@\"; do\n98\t      pfield=\"${pair%%=*}\"\n99\t      if [ \"$pfield\" = \"$field\" ]; then\n100\t        val=\"${pair#*=}\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4A9waHDHnQqf3E6JyWFYr","session_id":"69a482d3-3134-43a5-90c0-a00b8e39408a","uuid":"1f3db585-925e-4767-87d8-de6e207a3056","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:08:50.213Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Find friction in engine modules"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QYNg8XT2UX7NF8VpjQkaQC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Almanac\n2\t\n3\t## Skill Authoring\n4\t\n5\t**Naming.** `noun-verb` (`pr-create`, `ci-fix`, `session-recap`). Never `verb-noun` — breaks alphabetical grouping by topic. Lowercase alphanumeric + hyphens, no `--`, no leading/trailing `-`. Name must match directory exactly.\n6\t\n7\t**Categories.** Skills live nested at `skills/<category>/<name>/SKILL.md`. Current categories: `git/` (git/`gh` ops), `agents-md/` (CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md tooling), `loop/` (PRD → issues → autonomous ralph-loop), `comms/` (client/team-facing comms — emails, release notes, etc.), `other/`. Add new categories freely — validator just walks the tree. Names must be unique across the whole tree (validator hard-fails on collisions). The category is purely organizational; install-time symlinks flatten everything to `~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>` because Claude Code skill discovery is flat (only direct children of the skills dir are scanned).\n8\t\n9\t**Format.** Description must start with `Use when` and state the trigger explicitly — agents under-trigger otherwise. Body under 500 lines; move detail to `references/`. Full frontmatter schema: `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n10\t\n11\t**Description length.** Hard cap **220 chars** (validator enforces). State the trigger once — do **not** restate it as `Use this whenever the user says X, Y, Z, or wants to A`. Mechanism details (subagent counts, internal modes, scoring rubrics) belong in the SKILL.md body, not frontmatter. The aggregated skills listing is loaded into every Claude session — bloated descriptions burn tokens and risk getting truncated/dropped.\n12\t\n13\t**Manual-only skills.** Set `disable-model-invocation: true` in frontmatter to strip a skill from the auto-listing entirely (saves ~200 chars/skill in the listing). The skill stays user-invocable via `/almanac:<name>` and orchestrators can still load it by path. Use sparingly — only for skills with no plausible natural-language trigger at all. Default is auto-invocable: any skill whose trigger phrase a user might say (`branch-name`, `ci-fix`, `commit`, `push`, `pr-create`, `commits-squash`, `complexity-assess`, `diagnose`, etc.) must auto-fire.\n14\t\n15\t## Decision Framework: New Skill vs Extend\n16\t\n17\tBefore creating a new skill: (1) check if an existing skill covers the trigger, (2) if it overlaps an orchestrator (`ship`, `task-start`), extend the orchestrator instead of adding a sibling.\n18\t\n19\t## Skill Deduplication\n20\t\n21\tOrchestrators (`ship`, `task-start`) **must** reference standalone skills, never inline their logic. One source of truth per capability:\n22\t\n23\t- **Branch naming** → `branch-name`\n24\t- **Committing** → `commit`\n25\t- **Pushing** → `push`\n26\t- **PR creation** → `pr-create`\n27\t- **Complexity scoring** → `complexity-assess`\n28\t- **Rebasing** → `rebase`\n29\t\n30\tDelegate with \"Follow the `<skill-name>` skill\" — never duplicate. Declare hard deps in `metadata.dependencies`; validation fails if a listed dep doesn't exist.\n31\t\n32\t## Code Organization\n33\t\n34\t**Validation.** Always extend `almanac_validate_skill()` in `lib/almanac-core.sh` for new skill-format rules. Never inline ad-hoc checks in `tests/test-skills.sh` — leads to scattered validation. Always run `bash tests/test-skills.sh` after editing any skill; if it fails, fix the skill — never skip the test, weaken the validator, or commit with failures.\n35\t\n36\t**Test split.** `tests/test-structure.sh` validates layout (skill dirs, required files, CLI scripts present); `tests/test-skills.sh` validates skill *contents* via `almanac_validate_skill()`. Extend the right one — layout rules go in structure, content/format rules go in skills.\n37\t\n38\t**CLI helpers.** `cmd/*.sh` scripts must source `lib/core.sh` and use `_die`/`_info`/`_success`/`_warn`/`_error` — never `echo` errors directly or roll new helpers. Existing offenders to NOT pattern-match from: `cmd/list.sh` and `cmd/sync.sh` both predate this rule and still use raw `echo`.\n39\t\n40\t**Where helpers go.** Skill-format helpers (frontmatter parsing, `almanac_validate_skill`, anything `tests/test-skills.sh` calls) in `lib/almanac-core.sh`; CLI/output helpers (`_die`/`_info`/colors, anything `cmd/*.sh` or `install.sh` calls) in `lib/core.sh`. If unclear, ask whether non-skill code (CLI, install, sync) needs it — yes → `core.sh`; no → `almanac-core.sh`. Don't cross-contaminate.\n41\t\n42\t## Symlink Map\n43\t\n44\t- **In-repo:** `providers/claude-code/skills` → `../../skills`. The plugin distribution path is no longer maintained (skills are now nested at `skills/<category>/<name>` and Claude Code's plugin loader expects flat). The symlink stays for backward-compat but the install CLI is the supported install path.\n45\t- **Install-time, per skill (slash command):** `~/.claude/commands/almanac/<name>.md` → each skill's `SKILL.md`. Single-file symlink — never resolve `scripts/` or `references/` from this path.\n46\t- **Install-time, per skill (resources):** `~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>` → `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/<category>/<name>` (directory symlink). One per skill, flattening the categorized layout. Always resolve runnable assets from here: `~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>/scripts/...`.\n47\t- **Install-time, global config:** `providers/claude-code/CLAUDE.md` → `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` when user passes `--global-config`. Edits affect every Claude Code session globally — keep project-only rules in this file, not there.\n48\t\n49\tChanges to install symlink layout go in `cmd/install.sh` — don't fork the logic into another script. Helpers `almanac_list_skills`, `almanac_find_skill`, `almanac_validate_unique_names` (in `lib/almanac-core.sh`) are the only sanctioned ways to walk the skills tree — never `for d in skills/*/`.\n50\t\n51\t## Skill Resources (scripts/, references/)\n52\t\n53\t**Always print absolute paths under `~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>/scripts/...` in user-facing instructions. Never use `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/...`** — the commands path is a single-file symlink and won't resolve subdirs. Only the per-skill directory symlinks at `~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>` resolve.\n54\t\n55\tExisting offenders to NOT pattern-match from: `codebase-improve`, `diagnose`, `tdd`, `task-start`, `grill-me`, `agents-md-audit`, `ralph-loop` — all still reference `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/...`.\n56\t\n57\t## Doc Sync\n58\t\n59\tWhen adding/removing/renaming a skill, you **must** update in the same commit:\n60\t\n61\t- `README.md` — skills table, structure diagram, sync example\n62\t- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`\n63\t- `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`\n64\t\n65\t`test-structure.sh` only catches missing skill files — not stale prose. Adapted skills track upstream sources via `metadata.upstream-sha`; `almanac sync` checks for updates.\n66\t\n67\t## Self-Maintenance\n68\t\n69\tExisting code may predate a rule. Check this file first; don't pattern-match from `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` paths or direct `echo` in `cmd/*.sh` — both violate rules above. Discovered an undocumented gotcha? Add it here in the same commit.\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AYNdbHLKcs4aZiRjRox6Fj","session_id":"69a482d3-3134-43a5-90c0-a00b8e39408a","uuid":"ca8da4bd-1c7e-4248-a92a-4fb431b5b8ad","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:08:50.338Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Find friction in CLI/tests"}
