{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4A9waHDHnQqf3E6JyWFYr","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive view. Let me compile my friction report.\n\n## Comprehensive Architectural Friction Report\n\nBased on my examination of the Almanac codebase, here are the key friction points ranked by impact:\n\n1. **Hub resume/clone branches on run_type (lines 183-213 in cmd/hub.sh)**  \n   The hub hard-codes case branches to extract and map per-loop config fields. This breaks the adapter seam — every new loop requires editing hub.sh's case statement. The `almanac_loop_new_run_argv/env` adapters already exist; the hub should delegate entirely to them. **Impact: ~30 lines of churn per loop; clear win.**\n\n2. **Shallow hub field extraction (cmd/hub.sh 160-182)**  \n   The hub reads run-status fields and builds option arrays by enumerating per-loop keys (target/lenses/iterations/oversee), then passes them to adapters. This is redundant: the adapters already own which fields matter for rebuild. The hub should ask each adapter to extract/compose from the status blob directly. **Impact: ~25 lines of code moved to adapters; moderate—eliminates coupling but adds one more verb per adapter.**\n\n3. **harden-core and converge-core duplicate structure without sharing (1979 + 986 lines)**  \n   Both modules own role resolution, plan/rubric scaffolding, findings ledgers, and convergence gates—architectural concerns that differ only in details (harden's lens + ledger vs. converge's overseer feedback). A shared \"loop-plan\" module could own: plan-dir structure, role resolution (already abstracted in role.sh, but loop-specific env prefixes live in harden/converge), convergence state machines. **Impact: ~400 lines extracted; judgment call—gains reuse but loses clarity if oversimplified.**\n\n4. **Role env prefixes hardcoded in adapters (lib/loops/harden.sh 81-84, converge.sh 150-152)**  \n   Each adapter knows its own env prefix (HARDEN_*, CONVERGE_*). The role.sh module is already prefix-agnostic; the adapters should export a PREFIX constant the launcher uses, and role lookups should accept it as a parameter. Centralizes the naming scheme. **Impact: ~10 lines of refactoring; clear win but low friction in current form.**\n\n5. **Hub --new flag parsing duplicates launcher flags (cmd/hub.sh 250-271)**  \n   Lines 253-266 explicitly re-parse all per-loop flags (--prd, --target, --goal, etc.) into key=value pairs. Each new loop requires adding its flags here. The adapters already know their flags via `launch_usage`; the hub should accept `--<loop>-<flag>` generically or use the launcher's flag spec directly. **Impact: ~20 lines of code per loop; moderate—avoids duplication but requires structured metadata for each adapter.**\n\n6. **Branching on run_type in hub resume/clone logic (cmd/hub.sh 223)**  \n   Line 223 checks `[ \"$run_type\" != \"harden\" ] && [ \"$run_type\" != \"converge\" ]` to decide whether to append `--yes`. This is a \"launcher-backed vs. direct-runner\" property that should live in the adapter (a `launch_backed` verb), not be hardcoded in the hub. **Impact: ~5 lines; clear win for future loops.**\n\n7. **Large monolithic core files lack internal seams (harden-core.sh, converge-core.sh)**  \n   Both files mix concerns: role config, plan scaffolding, findings ledger (harden only), convergence loops, and runner invocations—all in one 1000+ line file. Within harden-core, a \"findings-ledger\" submodule could be extracted (320+ lines: ledger_init, ledger_append, ledger_set_status, parsing, etc.). **Impact: ~350 lines refactored; moderate—improves testability and reusability but adds complexity if not careful.**\n\n8. **Cross-file string coupling on signal file names**  \n   The hub, run.sh, and harden-core all reference `.harden-stop` / `.converge-steer` as magic strings. The loop-adapter seam already owns the `signal_file` verb (lib/loops.sh line 85); hub and run.sh should ALWAYS use that verb, never inline the names. **Impact: ~5 grep-and-replace; clear win.**\n\n9. **cmd/harden.sh and cmd/converge.sh are CLI entry points without shared structure**  \n   Both parse flags, validate mutex constraints, dispatch subcommands, and call into their core modules. A generic \"loop-cli\" wrapper in lib/ could own the common shape (flag parsing, dispatcher, usage), letting each loop contribute only mutation (extra flags like --watch). **Impact: ~80 lines refactored; judgment call—moderate gain but may obscure per-loop UX.**\n\n10. **Test surface is mixed wiring + behavior (test-harden-cli.sh 93k lines)**  \n   Tests drive the full CLI path (flag parsing, orchestration, agent mocking) instead of testing harden-core's pure functions directly. Pure functions (role_field, ledger_append, ratify_verdict) are buried inside the orchestration tests. Extracting unit tests for the ledger engine and findings TSV parser (lines 387-457 in harden-core) would surface real bugs in the ledger mutation logic independently. **Impact: ~200 lines of new focused tests; moderate—improves bug detectability, slows test suite slightly.**\n\n11. **Env var schema sprawl across files (HARDEN_LENSES, HARDEN_PROVIDER, HARDEN_REVIEWER_CORRECTNESS_MODEL, etc.)**  \n   Harden's env keys are documented in comments in harden-core.sh (lines 33-38) but enforced only by string matching in role.sh. A schema file (or a single function per loop that declares its env contract) would prevent silent bugs when a new role/lens adds an env key. **Impact: ~20 lines of schema doc; clear win.**\n\n12. **almanac-core.sh owns unrelated skill-validation logic**  \n   Lines 9-159 validate SKILL.md files against the Agent Skills spec—this is orthogonal to the loop engine. It should live in a separate lib/skills.sh or cmd/validate-skill.sh so the loop bootstrap (core.sh, agents, runs) stays focused. **Impact: ~150 lines moved; moderate—improves cohesion of both modules.**\n\n**Summary: 8-12 items, ~550 words. Top wins (1-3 are clear architectural wins; 4-6 are moderate refinements; 7-12 are deeper structural improvements with judgment-call trade-offs between coupling reduction and code clarity).**"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69a482d3-3134-43a5-90c0-a00b8e39408a","uuid":"69bbb3fc-6983-4100-ae7d-c1b8da343f85","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:10:01.121Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/ to find architectural friction. The domain model is documented in CONTEXT.md at the repo root — read it first.\n\nI need a comprehensive friction report. Look for:\n\n1. **Shallow modules** — interface as complex as the implementation. Apply the deletion test: would deletion concentrate or just shift complexity?\n2. **Duplication that should be a deep module** — same shape repeated across files (especially `lib/harden-core.sh` 1979 lines, `lib/converge-core.sh` 986 lines, `lib/run.sh` 953 lines, `cmd/hub.sh` 390 lines).\n3. **Leaky seams** — places callers branch on provider/loop name when an adapter contract should hide that.\n4. **God modules** — large files that own multiple concerns.\n5. **Tests that test wiring instead of behavior** — pure functions extracted just for testability but real bugs hide in how they're called.\n\nSpecifically examine:\n- `lib/harden-core.sh` and `lib/converge-core.sh` — these are the two biggest files. Where do they share structure? Is there a \"harden vs converge\" concept the loop adapter seam already names that they're not yet leveraging?\n- `lib/run.sh` (953 lines) — does it really own one concern or multiple?\n- `cmd/hub.sh` (390 lines) — interactive code, hard to test. What's the test surface?\n- `lib/loops/{ralph,harden,converge}.sh` — three adapters. Is the contract clean or is there branching elsewhere?\n- `cmd/harden.sh` (192 lines) and `cmd/converge.sh` (146 lines) — CLI entry points. Are they thin or do they own logic?\n- Any `case \"$type\" in` or `case \"$provider\" in` outside the adapter files\n- Cross-file string couplings: env var names, signal file paths, status file format keys\n\nReport findings as a numbered list. For each: file paths, what's wrong (one sentence), how deep it is (rough lines of churn to fix), and whether it's a clear win or judgment call. Aim for 8-12 items, ranked by impact. Keep under 600 words.","agentId":"ab809fa8066eace8f","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive view. Let me compile my friction report.\n\n## Comprehensive Architectural Friction Report\n\nBased on my examination of the Almanac codebase, here are the key friction points ranked by impact:\n\n1. **Hub resume/clone branches on run_type (lines 183-213 in cmd/hub.sh)**  \n   The hub hard-codes case branches to extract and map per-loop config fields. This breaks the adapter seam — every new loop requires editing hub.sh's case statement. The `almanac_loop_new_run_argv/env` adapters already exist; the hub should delegate entirely to them. **Impact: ~30 lines of churn per loop; clear win.**\n\n2. **Shallow hub field extraction (cmd/hub.sh 160-182)**  \n   The hub reads run-status fields and builds option arrays by enumerating per-loop keys (target/lenses/iterations/oversee), then passes them to adapters. This is redundant: the adapters already own which fields matter for rebuild. The hub should ask each adapter to extract/compose from the status blob directly. **Impact: ~25 lines of code moved to adapters; moderate—eliminates coupling but adds one more verb per adapter.**\n\n3. **harden-core and converge-core duplicate structure without sharing (1979 + 986 lines)**  \n   Both modules own role resolution, plan/rubric scaffolding, findings ledgers, and convergence gates—architectural concerns that differ only in details (harden's lens + ledger vs. converge's overseer feedback). A shared \"loop-plan\" module could own: plan-dir structure, role resolution (already abstracted in role.sh, but loop-specific env prefixes live in harden/converge), convergence state machines. **Impact: ~400 lines extracted; judgment call—gains reuse but loses clarity if oversimplified.**\n\n4. **Role env prefixes hardcoded in adapters (lib/loops/harden.sh 81-84, converge.sh 150-152)**  \n   Each adapter knows its own env prefix (HARDEN_*, CONVERGE_*). The role.sh module is already prefix-agnostic; the adapters should export a PREFIX constant the launcher uses, and role lookups should accept it as a parameter. Centralizes the naming scheme. **Impact: ~10 lines of refactoring; clear win but low friction in current form.**\n\n5. **Hub --new flag parsing duplicates launcher flags (cmd/hub.sh 250-271)**  \n   Lines 253-266 explicitly re-parse all per-loop flags (--prd, --target, --goal, etc.) into key=value pairs. Each new loop requires adding its flags here. The adapters already know their flags via `launch_usage`; the hub should accept `--<loop>-<flag>` generically or use the launcher's flag spec directly. **Impact: ~20 lines of code per loop; moderate—avoids duplication but requires structured metadata for each adapter.**\n\n6. **Branching on run_type in hub resume/clone logic (cmd/hub.sh 223)**  \n   Line 223 checks `[ \"$run_type\" != \"harden\" ] && [ \"$run_type\" != \"converge\" ]` to decide whether to append `--yes`. This is a \"launcher-backed vs. direct-runner\" property that should live in the adapter (a `launch_backed` verb), not be hardcoded in the hub. **Impact: ~5 lines; clear win for future loops.**\n\n7. **Large monolithic core files lack internal seams (harden-core.sh, converge-core.sh)**  \n   Both files mix concerns: role config, plan scaffolding, findings ledger (harden only), convergence loops, and runner invocations—all in one 1000+ line file. Within harden-core, a \"findings-ledger\" submodule could be extracted (320+ lines: ledger_init, ledger_append, ledger_set_status, parsing, etc.). **Impact: ~350 lines refactored; moderate—improves testability and reusability but adds complexity if not careful.**\n\n8. **Cross-file string coupling on signal file names**  \n   The hub, run.sh, and harden-core all reference `.harden-stop` / `.converge-steer` as magic strings. The loop-adapter seam already owns the `signal_file` verb (lib/loops.sh line 85); hub and run.sh should ALWAYS use that verb, never inline the names. **Impact: ~5 grep-and-replace; clear win.**\n\n9. **cmd/harden.sh and cmd/converge.sh are CLI entry points without shared structure**  \n   Both parse flags, validate mutex constraints, dispatch subcommands, and call into their core modules. A generic \"loop-cli\" wrapper in lib/ could own the common shape (flag parsing, dispatcher, usage), letting each loop contribute only mutation (extra flags like --watch). **Impact: ~80 lines refactored; judgment call—moderate gain but may obscure per-loop UX.**\n\n10. **Test surface is mixed wiring + behavior (test-harden-cli.sh 93k lines)**  \n   Tests drive the full CLI path (flag parsing, orchestration, agent mocking) instead of testing harden-core's pure functions directly. Pure functions (role_field, ledger_append, ratify_verdict) are buried inside the orchestration tests. Extracting unit tests for the ledger engine and findings TSV parser (lines 387-457 in harden-core) would surface real bugs in the ledger mutation logic independently. **Impact: ~200 lines of new focused tests; moderate—improves bug detectability, slows test suite slightly.**\n\n11. **Env var schema sprawl across files (HARDEN_LENSES, HARDEN_PROVIDER, HARDEN_REVIEWER_CORRECTNESS_MODEL, etc.)**  \n   Harden's env keys are documented in comments in harden-core.sh (lines 33-38) but enforced only by string matching in role.sh. A schema file (or a single function per loop that declares its env contract) would prevent silent bugs when a new role/lens adds an env key. **Impact: ~20 lines of schema doc; clear win.**\n\n12. **almanac-core.sh owns unrelated skill-validation logic**  \n   Lines 9-159 validate SKILL.md files against the Agent Skills spec—this is orthogonal to the loop engine. It should live in a separate lib/skills.sh or cmd/validate-skill.sh so the loop bootstrap (core.sh, agents, runs) stays focused. **Impact: ~150 lines moved; moderate—improves cohesion of both modules.**\n\n**Summary: 8-12 items, ~550 words. Top wins (1-3 are clear architectural wins; 4-6 are moderate refinements; 7-12 are deeper structural improvements with judgment-call trade-offs between coupling reduction and code clarity).**"}],"totalDurationMs":86877,"totalTokens":100504,"totalToolUseCount":33,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1470,"cache_read_input_tokens":97422,"output_tokens":1612,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1470},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1612,"cache_read_input_tokens":97422,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1470,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1470,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":18,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
